 ## What the report measures
 
-The report records independently supplied category/type labels, recognized category/type, automatic versus category-review outcomes, bounded scanner rejection diagnostics, error codes and elapsed times. It includes the release identifier and recognition-vector SHA256 to identify the version tested. Personal clothing names, file names, photos, data URLs, account identifiers, email addresses, raw exceptions and arbitrary diagnostic text are excluded.
+The report records independently supplied category/type/color labels, recognized category/type/color, the color source (photo estimate or recognition), automatic versus category-review outcomes, bounded scanner rejection diagnostics, error codes and elapsed times. It includes the release identifier and recognition-vector SHA256 to identify the version tested. Personal clothing names, file names, photos, data URLs, account identifiers, email addresses, raw exceptions and arbitrary diagnostic text are excluded.
+
+Report format version 2 adds primary-color and joint category/color counts. `coreChecked` includes every color-labeled garment, including runtime failures; `coreMatches` requires both an accepted correct category and the correct color. `automaticCoreChecked` counts automatic identifications among those garments, with `automaticCoreMatches` and `incorrectAutomaticCore` reporting correct and incorrect pairs. Read automatic precision and automatic coverage together; zero automatic identifications is not a successful accuracy result. Historical labels without colors remain supported and are excluded from color/joint counts.
+
+The check uses the production `checkPhoto` color sample and `ClothingDetails` merge rules for single-garment imports: a valid recognized color supersedes the photo estimate only after category acceptance. This does not validate whole-outfit masks, automatic crop completeness, wardrobe saving, or physical-device hardware.
 
 Category and subtype accuracy are separate. A confirmed dress category with Unknown type counts as a category match and a type miss when the user supplied a type. Unlabeled types are excluded from type scoring. Runtime failures remain in the denominator rather than disappearing from the report. Incorrect automatic categories and types are explicitly counted; nonclothing rejections are scored separately.
 
